newaqemu — Qt6 graphical frontend for QEMU
==========================================

newaqemu is a fork of AQEMU aimed at modern Linux and Windows hosts. It keeps
the familiar AQEMU workflow while adding a Qt6/C++17 codebase, hybrid QEMU /
libvirt backends, and optional GPU passthrough host integration (Linux only).

For a feature-oriented overview, see README.md in this source tree.


PLATFORMS
---------

  Linux   — full feature set (ordinary VMs, passthrough, host setup helper)
  Windows — build supported; GPU passthrough remains Linux-only


MINIMUM DEPENDENCIES
--------------------

  Qt6        — Core, Widgets, Network, Test, PrintSupport
  CMake      — >= 3.16
  C++17      — compiler with C++17 support (GCC, Clang, or MSVC on Windows)
  QEMU       — >= 6.x (8.x+ recommended)

  Linux passthrough profile (optional):
    libvirt and virsh (WITH_LIBVIRT=on, default)

  Optional:
    LibVNCServer     — embedded VNC display (omit with WITHOUT_EMBEDDED_DISPLAY=on)
    virt-viewer or remote-viewer — SPICE display helper
    looking-glass-client, kvmfr, i915-sriov-dkms — Intel iGPU VF workflow (Linux)


UPGRADING FROM AQEMU
--------------------

  newaqemu installs as a separate application (binary: newaqemu, data under
  share/newaqemu, user config under ~/.config/newaqemu/). On first run it can
  import legacy AQEMU settings and .aqemu VM definitions.

  If you previously installed AQEMU under /usr/share/aqemu or
  /usr/share/apps/aqemu, that tree is not removed automatically when you install
  newaqemu elsewhere; delete old AQEMU resource directories manually if you no
  longer need them.

  After importing VMs, open each VM and click Apply once if prompted so stored
  settings match the current format.


BUILD AND INSTALL (Linux)
-------------------------

  1. Extract or clone the source, then:

       cd newaqemu
       make
       sudo make install

     Embedded VNC + SPICE (requires LibVNCServer):

       make full
       sudo make install

     Run "make help" for other targets and variables (PREFIX, JOBS, CMAKE_ARGS).

  2. Run:  newaqemu

  Common CMake options:

    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=PATH
        Install prefix (default: /usr/local). Binaries go to PREFIX/bin,
        data to PREFIX/share/newaqemu.

    -DWITHOUT_EMBEDDED_DISPLAY=on
        Build without embedded VNC (no LibVNCServer required).

    -DWITH_SPICE_DISPLAY=on|off  (default: on)
        SPICE helper using external remote-viewer (only if embedded display on).

    -DWITH_LIBVIRT=on|off  (default: on)
        Compile libvirt passthrough backend support.

    -DDEBUG=on
        Debug build (-Wall -g).

    -DINSTALL_MAN=on|off  (default: on)
        Install newaqemu.1 man page.

    -DUPDATE_TRANSLATIONS=on
        Regenerate translation sources (developers only).

  Minimal build is the default for "make" (no embedded display).

  Explicit CMake (same as default "make"):

       cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DWITHOUT_EMBEDDED_DISPLAY=on
       cmake --build build
       sudo cmake --install build


BUILD (Windows)
---------------

  Use Qt6 for Windows (MSVC or MinGW), then:

       cmake -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<Qt6_DIR>
       cmake --build build
       windeployqt build/newaqemu.exe

  Use TCP monitor (default on Windows). User-mode NAT works without extra
  drivers; TAP bridging needs a separate TAP-Windows adapter (not automated).


GPU PASSTHROUGH (Linux only)
------------------------------

  1. Advanced Settings → GPU passthrough — review host readiness.
  2. Apply host templates, e.g.:
       pkexec newaqemu-host-setup --gpu-bdf 0000:00:02.1 --kvmfr-mb 32
  3. Set kernel cmdline (intel_iommu=on, i915.max_vfs=1, …) and reboot.
  4. Create a VM with GPU passthrough enabled in the wizard.
  5. After Windows install: Intel driver, Looking Glass host, VDD, then switch
     display to Looking Glass as documented in-app.

  Reference workflow (external):
  https://pen.waltuh.cyou/yonle/iommu-with-sr-iov-gpu-accelerated-windows-vm-on-a-linux-laptop-that-has-only


EMBEDDED VNC / SPICE
--------------------

  Embedded VNC is optional at build time and can be experimental; prefer
  external viewers if you hit display issues. SPICE opens remote-viewer or
  virt-viewer when SPICE is enabled on the VM and WITH_SPICE_DISPLAY is on.


